Morning,

I need help with something that sounds simple but probably is not.

Product needs to define successful channel adoption as sustained useful activ=
ity rather than enrolment or one event. Several teams are reporting more fail=
ed or retried activity than they expected, but they do not agree on the cause=
. There are enough data exceptions to make a polished average dangerous; pres=
erve the unresolved cases.

The questions I would ask in the room are:
1. What would we expect to see if adoption differs by product and customer ne=
ed?
2. What evidence would instead support that customers return to assisted chan=
nels after failure?
3. How do we rule out that first successful use predicts continued use?

This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not strong enoug=
h to become a standing rule. Use May 2023 as the new evidence point rather th=
an recycling the earlier conclusion. Please work towards which onboarding jou=
rney and success metric should change. A useful output would be a short decis=
ion pack with no more than five slides and an attached evidence table.

Use monthly account snapshots, status events, limits, product enrolments and =
signatories; transaction-level timestamps, channels, amounts, statuses and er=
ror context; customer contacts, complaints and suggestions; ATM attempts, hos=
t responses, PIN attempts, blocked-card state and latency. One caution: Avail=
able data supports channel behaviour but not every app screen, click or aband=
oned session.

Regards
